@@ -0,0 +1,156 @@
+@page fatras_hadronic Table-driven hadronic interactions
+
+@ref ActsFatras::NuclearInteraction is an optional inelastic interaction model for
+charged pions and protons. It samples complete correlated final states from an
+immutable @ref ActsFatras::NuclearInteractionTable. Geant4 is not needed to build
+or run this model. Tables must be calibrated externally; ACTS does not supply a
+production calibration or invent a default multiplicity distribution.
+
+## Model and domain
+
+The table separates interaction rates from conditional final states. For a slab
+of nuclear thickness `t = thicknessInL0()`, the interaction probability is
+`-expm1(-ratePerL0 * t)`. Independent sampling on split slabs gives the same
+survival probability when the material and momentum remain constant. The rate
+must use the same nuclear-length convention as the material map. A calibration
+from a macroscopic cross section uses `ratePerL0 = crossSectionPerLength * L0`.
+
+The process uses the material-aware surface-process interface, after the charged
+EM processes in @ref ActsFatras::StandardChargedInteractions. A successful
+interaction terminates the parent with `SimulationOutcome::KilledInteraction`,
+retains its final momentum for truth bookkeeping and creates daughters at its
+four-position. The usual secondary transport and hit generation then apply.
+Vertices lie on the tracking surface; EM effects are applied over the incoming
+slab before the nuclear interaction. This is a thin-surface approximation. It
+does not model an interaction depth or a shower developing inside a thick slab.
+
+Material lookup matches `Ar` and `Z` to relative tolerance 1e-3. It does not replace
+an uncalibrated material with a nearby element. Momentum lookup selects the lower
+node for the event library and linearly interpolates the rate in momentum
+between adjacent nodes. A zero-rate lower node disables that interval, so a
+higher-energy final-state library is not borrowed below its domain. The domain is
+closed at both ends, and no extrapolation is performed. Unsupported species,
+materials and momenta produce no nuclear interaction and consume no random
+numbers. A null table disables the model. Coverage of the actual material map
+and input spectrum must therefore be checked before interpreting occupancy.
+
+One complete event is selected uniformly. All outgoing centre-of-mass momenta,
+including an untransported residual system, are scaled by a common factor so that
+the sum of on-shell energies equals the actual projectile-plus-target collision
+energy. This preserves the sampled directions, multiplicity and momentum
+balance. The event is randomly rotated about the incident direction and boosted
+to the laboratory frame. The lower-node choice avoids borrowing a higher-energy
+channel below its production threshold. Complete absorption is represented by
+an empty product list; the residual system receives the incident four-momentum
+plus the target rest energy. Its excitation can change with incident momentum.
+
+This mapping is an approximation to energy dependence, particularly near
+thresholds and with coarse momentum grids. Multiplicity distributions are
+piecewise constant and may jump at nodes. Use independent reference samples
+between nodes to choose grid density and quantify this bias.
+
+The target mass and residual four-momentum are essential: the visible particles
+do **not** conserve the projectile's four-momentum alone. Table construction
+checks electric charge and CM four-momentum conservation, but does not validate a
+nuclear isotope assignment or baryon content for the aggregate residual system.
+Its definition and the treatment of nuclear fragments/local energy deposits
+belong to the calibration provenance. The residual system is not transported or
+written as a simulation particle. It is available in the low-level sampled
+result for conservation checks.
+
+Prompt pi0s are replaced by two isotropic photons in the pion rest frame, with
+exact two-body kinematics and a laboratory boost. This prompt vertex is folded
+into the nuclear interaction, so those photons have `eNuclearInteraction` and
+can subsequently convert with the existing neutral EM model. Other unstable
+products are subject to the configured decay module; the Examples simulator
+still uses `NoDecay`. Neutral hadron interactions, other projectile species,
+at-rest capture and detailed nuclear-fragment transport are outside this model.
+
+## C++ configuration
+
+```cpp
+std::ifstream input("charged-hadrons.table");
+auto table = std::make_shared<const ActsFatras::NuclearInteractionTable>(
+    ActsFatras::NuclearInteractionTable::read(input));
+auto interactions = ActsFatras::makeStandardChargedInteractions(pMin, table);
+```
+
+The simulation's interaction-list type must be
+`ActsFatras::StandardChargedInteractions`. A simulator using
+`StandardChargedElectroMagneticInteractions` retains its EM-only interface.
+The table is shared read-only across particles and threads. All runtime random
+numbers come from the event generator. Daughter momentum cuts remain the
+responsibility of the simulator, not the calibration loader.
+
+## Version 1 table format
+
+The format is whitespace-delimited text, without comments or trailing tokens.
+All masses and momentum components are in GeV; charges are in elementary-charge
+units. The parser converts these to native ACTS units. A record count may not
+exceed one million. Invalid numeric values, unordered nodes, ambiguous channels,
+unsupported projectiles and nonconserving events throw `std::invalid_argument`.
+
+```text
+ACTS_FATRAS_NUCLEAR_V1
+numberOfChannels
+```
+
+For each channel:
+
+```text
+projectilePDG projectileMass materialAr materialZ numberOfMomentumNodes
+```
+
+For each momentum node (strictly increasing, at least two per channel):
+
+```text
+projectileMomentum ratePerL0 numberOfEvents
+```
+
+For each event:
+
+```text
+targetMass remnantMass remnantCharge remnantPx remnantPy remnantPz numberOfProducts
+```
+
+For each product:
+
+```text
+PDG mass charge px py pz
+```
+
+Product and residual momenta are in the projectile-target CM frame, with the
+incident projectile along +z. Each target is initially at rest in the lab.
+Natural isotope mixtures can have different target masses in different events.
+The target nuclear charge is the channel's material Z. Supported product PDGs
+must have mass and charge entries in ACTS; table values permit small differences
+between reference particle-data versions. An event must balance four-momentum
+to relative tolerance 1e-6 and charge to absolute tolerance 1e-6. Nonzero rates
+require nonempty event libraries. A zero rate may have no events.
+
+Keep a sidecar manifest with generator/physics-list version, particle-data and
+material definitions, seed, sampling cuts, nuclear-length convention, table
+checksum, and the training/validation sample split. Never silently drop
+nonconserving reference events to obtain a loadable table.
+
+## Validation before use
+
+Check rates and primary survival independently from conditional final states.
+Compare charged multiplicity, daughter momenta and angles, secondary hits by
+layer, generation depth, navigation failures and transport CPU. Include input
+momenta between grid nodes, backward-going daughters and the configured p/pT
+cuts. Tests with analytic final states check the implementation; they do not
+establish agreement with full simulation.
+
+Geant4 likewise separates the cross-section calculation from the final-state
+model; see its [hadronic physics description](https://geant4.web.cern.ch/documentation/dev/bftd_html/ForToolkitDeveloper/GuideToExtendFunctionality/HadronicPhysics/hadronics.html).
+The reference model is an offline calibration choice, not a runtime dependency.
+
+## Decay roadmap
+
+The replaceable Fatras decay interface does not require Geant4. A native module
+can sample proper lifetimes, branching fractions and exact two-body kinematics,
+starting with K-short and Lambda/anti-Lambda. More complicated channels require
+appropriate multi-body and angular distributions, with polarization handled
+explicitly when relevant. `Geant4Decay` can remain an optional reference adapter.
+This is separate from the prompt pi0 handling included in the nuclear process.
